/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 2 Samuel chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("2samuel", [
{n:11, name:"David and Bathsheba", seal:{ref:"2 Samuel 11:27",verse:"And when the mourning was past, David sent and fetched her to his house, and she became his wife, and bare him a son. But the thing that David had done displeased the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know David's fall began with staying home at the time when kings go forth to battle, a season when idleness met unchecked power (2 Samuel 11:1)?","Did you know Uriah, a Hittite, refused the comforts of home while the ark and his fellow soldiers slept in tents, showing more honor than the king who plotted against him (2 Samuel 11:11)?","Did you know the chapter's final sentence passes the verdict heaven had been holding back: the thing that David had done displeased the LORD (2 Samuel 11:27)?"], questions:[
{q:"At what season did David send Joab out while he stayed in Jerusalem?", options:["At the time when kings go forth to battle","At the wheat harvest","At the new moon feast","At the feast of tabernacles"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:1",verse:"And it came to pass, after the year was expired, at the time when kings go forth to battle, that David sent Joab, and his servants with him, and all Israel; and they destroyed the children of Ammon, and besieged Rabbah. But David tarried still at Jerusalem.", insight:"Idle hours at the height of power; the battle David skipped became the one he fought within himself."},
{q:"Where was David when he first saw the woman?", options:["Walking upon the roof of the king's house","Sitting at the city gate","Standing in the temple court","Riding through the valley"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:2",verse:"And it came to pass in an eveningtide, that David arose from off his bed, and walked upon the roof of the king’s house: and from the roof he saw a woman washing herself; and the woman was very beautiful to look upon.", insight:"An evening walk on the roof; small wanderings of the eyes grow into great wanderings of the heart."},
{q:"What was the woman doing when David saw her?", options:["Washing herself","Drawing water at the well","Grinding at the mill","Sleeping on her own roof"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:2",verse:"And it came to pass in an eveningtide, that David arose from off his bed, and walked upon the roof of the king’s house: and from the roof he saw a woman washing herself; and the woman was very beautiful to look upon.", insight:"The verse states it plainly; the sin was not in seeing but in sending for her afterward."},
{q:"What did David do after seeing the woman?", options:["Sent and inquired after the woman","Called Uriah home at once","Went down to her house himself","Prayed for deliverance"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:3",verse:"And David sent and enquired after the woman. And one said, Is not this Bath-sheba, the daughter of Eliam, the wife of Uriah the Hittite?", insight:"Inquiry is the hinge; desire entertained becomes desire pursued."},
{q:"What did the informant tell David about the woman?", options:["She is Bathsheba, the wife of Uriah the Hittite","She is a widow of the city","She is a servant in the palace","She is the daughter of Joab"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:3",verse:"And David sent and enquired after the woman. And one said, Is not this Bath-sheba, the daughter of Eliam, the wife of Uriah the Hittite?", insight:"He was warned she was another man's wife; knowledge of the boundary did not stop the trespass."},
{q:"After learning who she was, what did David do?", options:["Sent messengers, and took her","Wrote her a letter","Went to plead with Uriah","Turned away from the roof"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:4",verse:"And David sent messengers, and took her; and she came in unto him, and he lay with her; for she was purified from her uncleanness: and she returned unto her house.", insight:"Power made the sin easy; a king's summons is hard to refuse."},
{q:"What message did the woman afterward send to David?", options:["I am with child","My husband has returned","I am leaving Jerusalem","The king must repent"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:5",verse:"And the woman conceived, and sent and told David, and said, I am with child.", insight:"Sin sends its own messengers; consequences arrive uninvited."},
{q:"Whom did David order Joab to send to him?", options:["Uriah the Hittite","Abishai, the brother of Joab","The captain of the guard","Eliam, the woman's father"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:6",verse:"And David sent to Joab, saying, Send me Uriah the Hittite. And Joab sent Uriah to David.", insight:"The cover-up began with a summons; one sin hires another to hide it."},
{q:"When Uriah arrived, what did David ask him about?", options:["How Joab did, and how the people did, and how the war prospered","His family and his house","The ark and the priests","The tribute of the Ammonites"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:7",verse:"And when Uriah was come unto him, David demanded of him how Joab did, and how the people did, and how the war prospered.", insight:"Pleasant questions over an ugly plan; courtesy can be a mask for scheming."},
{q:"What did David urge Uriah to do?", options:["Go down to his house, and wash his feet","Return to the battle at once","Report to the elders of the city","Bring his wife to the palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:8",verse:"And David said to Uriah, Go down to thy house, and wash thy feet. And Uriah departed out of the king’s house, and there followed him a mess of meat from the king.", insight:"Wash thy feet sounds like kindness; it was a plot to hide another man's child."},
{q:"Where did Uriah spend that night instead of going home?", options:["At the door of the king's house, with the king's servants","In his own house with his wife","In the camp of Joab","At the house of Nathan"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:9",verse:"But Uriah slept at the door of the king’s house with all the servants of his lord, and went not down to his house.", insight:"Uriah's loyalty shamed the king's plotting; honor in a servant exposes dishonor in a master."},
{q:"Why did Uriah refuse to go down to his house?", options:["The ark and Israel abode in tents, and Joab and his servants were encamped in the open fields","He was angry with David","His house had been robbed","He feared the Ammonites at home"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:11",verse:"And Uriah said unto David, The ark, and Israel, and Judah, abide in tents; and my lord Joab, and the servants of my lord, are encamped in the open fields; shall I then go into mine house, to eat and to drink, and to lie with my wife? as thou livest, and as thy soul liveth, I will not do this thing.", insight:"Shall I then go into mine house, to eat and to drink? A Hittite's solidarity with his fellow soldiers outshone the king of Israel."},
{q:"How did David try again to send Uriah home?", options:["He made him drunk at his table","He threatened him with prison","He bribed him with gold","He put him under guard"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:13",verse:"And when David had called him, he did eat and drink before him; and he made him drunk: and at even he went out to lie on his bed with the servants of his lord, but went not down to his house.", insight:"Drunkenness was the next tool; when one scheme fails, sin reaches for a stronger one."},
{q:"What did David send to Joab in the morning?", options:["A letter, sent by the hand of Uriah","A gift of silver","Orders to end the siege","A pardon for the Ammonites"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:14",verse:"And it came to pass in the morning, that David wrote a letter to Joab, and sent it by the hand of Uriah.", insight:"The condemned man carried his own sentence; sin drafts even the innocent into its service."},
{q:"What did David's letter command Joab to do with Uriah?", options:["Set him in the forefront of the hottest battle, then retire from him","Promote him to captain","Send him home to Jerusalem","Place him over the baggage"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:15",verse:"And he wrote in the letter, saying, Set ye Uriah in the forefront of the hottest battle, and retire ye from him, that he may be smitten, and die.", insight:"Retire ye from him, that he may be smitten, and die; murder by arrangement, sealed with the king's own hand."},
{q:"Where did Joab assign Uriah?", options:["Where he knew that valiant men were","At the rear with the supplies","Upon the city wall","Inside the king's tent"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:16",verse:"And it came to pass, when Joab observed the city, that he assigned Uriah unto a place where he knew that valiant men were.", insight:"Joab obeyed the wicked order without question; loyalty to a king must never outrank loyalty to God."},
{q:"How did Uriah die?", options:["In battle, when the men of the city came out and fought with Joab","By the sword of Joab himself","From a plague in the camp","Falling from the city wall"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:17",verse:"And the men of the city went out, and fought with Joab: and there fell some of the people of the servants of David; and Uriah the Hittite died also.", insight:"Other servants fell with him that day; one man's sin spends other men's lives."},
{q:"What was the messenger told to add if David grew angry at the report?", options:["Thy servant Uriah the Hittite is dead also","Joab has taken the city","The ark is safe","The people are returning"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:21",verse:"Who smote Abimelech the son of Jerubbesheth? did not a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon him from the wall, that he died in Thebez? why went ye nigh the wall? then say thou, Thy servant Uriah the Hittite is dead also.", insight:"Even Joab knew the king would rage at the tactic; the excuse was prepared before the crime."},
{q:"How did David answer the news of Uriah's death?", options:["Let not this thing displease thee, for the sword devoureth one as well as another","Mourn for the fallen","Punish Joab at once","Send for the ark"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:25",verse:"Then David said unto the messenger, Thus shalt thou say unto Joab, Let not this thing displease thee, for the sword devoureth one as well as another: make thy battle more strong against the city, and overthrow it: and encourage thou him.", insight:"Cold comfort dressed as a proverb; David spoke of the sword as if it, and not he, had done the killing."},
{q:"How does the chapter end its account of David's deed?", options:["The thing that David had done displeased the LORD","David offered a sacrifice","The people praised the king","Joab was dismissed"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 11:27",verse:"And when the mourning was past, David sent and fetched her to his house, and she became his wife, and bare him a son. But the thing that David had done displeased the LORD.", insight:"Man's schemes succeeded, and heaven frowned; the last word belongs not to the king but to the LORD."}
]},
{n:12, name:"Nathan's Rebuke", seal:{ref:"2 Samuel 12:13",verse:"And David said unto Nathan, I have sinned against the LORD. And Nathan said unto David, The LORD also hath put away thy sin; thou shalt not die."}, facts:["Did you know David's own sentence on the rich man, death and fourfold repayment, became the measure of his own judgment (2 Samuel 12:5, 12:6)?","Did you know Nathan's rebuke, Thou art the man, is one of the shortest and sharpest in all Scripture, only four words in English (2 Samuel 12:7)?","Did you know the name Jedidiah, given to Solomon by the LORD through Nathan, means beloved of the LORD, grace renaming the child of scandal (2 Samuel 12:25)?"], questions:[
{q:"Whom did the LORD send to David?", options:["Nathan the prophet","Gad the seer","Zadok the priest","Samuel the judge"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:1",verse:"And the LORD sent Nathan unto David. And he came unto him, and said unto him, There were two men in one city; the one rich, and the other poor.", insight:"God sends His word to kings as well as commoners; no throne sits beyond rebuke."},
{q:"In Nathan's parable, what did the poor man own?", options:["One little ewe lamb","A single olive tree","A small vineyard","One servant"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:3",verse:"But the poor man had nothing, save one little ewe lamb, which he had bought and nourished up: and it grew up together with him, and with his children; it did eat of his own meat, and drank of his own cup, and lay in his bosom, and was unto him as a daughter.", insight:"The lamb ate of his meat and lay in his bosom; Nathan made David feel the poor man's love before naming the crime."},
{q:"What sentence did David pronounce on the rich man?", options:["He shall surely die","He shall pay a fine","He shall serve the poor man","He shall be exiled"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:5",verse:"And David’s anger was greatly kindled against the man; and he said to Nathan, As the LORD liveth, the man that hath done this thing shall surely die:", insight:"David's anger burned hot against a story; the conscience condemns in others what it excuses in itself."},
{q:"What restitution did David demand of the man?", options:["Fourfold, because he had no pity","Sevenfold","Double","Tenfold"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:6",verse:"And he shall restore the lamb fourfold, because he did this thing, and because he had no pity.", insight:"Because he had no pity; David named the very crime, pitylessness, that he had shown Uriah."},
{q:"What words did Nathan then speak to David?", options:["Thou art the man","Repent, O king","God has spoken","Hear the LORD"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:7",verse:"And Nathan said to David, Thou art the man. Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, I anointed thee king over Israel, and I delivered thee out of the hand of Saul;", insight:"Three words, and the parable turned into a mirror; God's word aims at the heart, not the crowd."},
{q:"What charge did God lay at David's door?", options:["Wherefore hast thou despised the commandment of the LORD?","Why hast thou forsaken the ark?","Where is thy brother?","Who told thee thou wast king?"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:9",verse:"Wherefore hast thou despised the commandment of the LORD, to do evil in his sight? thou hast killed Uriah the Hittite with the sword, and hast taken his wife to be thy wife, and hast slain him with the sword of the children of Ammon.", insight:"The charge was not only adultery and murder but despising God's word; sin always begins by thinking lightly of the command."},
{q:"What did God say would never depart from David's house?", options:["The sword","The famine","The plague","The tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:10",verse:"Now therefore the sword shall never depart from thine house; because thou hast despised me, and hast taken the wife of Uriah the Hittite to be thy wife.", insight:"David had killed by the sword of the Ammonites; now the sword would haunt his own house. Sown violence reaps violence."},
{q:"From where did God say He would raise up evil against David?", options:["Out of his own house","From the Philistines","From the Ammonites","From the house of Saul"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:11",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will raise up evil against thee out of thine own house, and I will take thy wives before thine eyes, and give them unto thy neighbour, and he shall lie with thy wives in the sight of this sun.", insight:"The wound would come from within; the house built by grace would be shaken by its own."},
{q:"What was David's confession?", options:["I have sinned against the LORD","Nathan, thou liest","I am the king","Let the child live"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:13",verse:"And David said unto Nathan, I have sinned against the LORD. And Nathan said unto David, The LORD also hath put away thy sin; thou shalt not die.", insight:"No excuses, no blame; five honest words opened the door to mercy."},
{q:"What mercy did Nathan announce to David?", options:["The LORD hath put away thy sin; thou shalt not die","The child shall surely live","The sword shall depart","The kingdom is taken from thee"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:13",verse:"And David said unto Nathan, I have sinned against the LORD. And Nathan said unto David, The LORD also hath put away thy sin; thou shalt not die.", insight:"Put away, not excused; forgiveness removes the guilt, though consequences may remain."},
{q:"Why did Nathan say the child would die?", options:["Because David's deed had given great occasion to the enemies of the LORD to blaspheme","Because the child was sickly","Because Bathsheba had sinned alone","Because Joab demanded it"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:14",verse:"Howbeit, because by this deed thou hast given great occasion to the enemies of the LORD to blaspheme, the child also that is born unto thee shall surely die.", insight:"A king's secret sin becomes the enemy's public taunt; our failures give scoffers their lines."},
{q:"What happened to the child after Nathan left?", options:["The LORD struck the child, and he was very sick","He was taken to Egypt","He was hidden by the nurses","He recovered at once"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:15",verse:"And Nathan departed unto his house. And the LORD struck the child that Uriah’s wife bare unto David, and it was very sick.", insight:"Nathan had barely left when judgment began; God's word does not return empty."},
{q:"What did David do while the child was sick?", options:["Besought God, and fasted, and lay all night upon the earth","Sent for the physicians","Offered burnt offerings","Called the elders to pray"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:16",verse:"David therefore besought God for the child; and David fasted, and went in, and lay all night upon the earth.", insight:"All night upon the earth; even a guilty father can pray like this, and David did."},
{q:"On which day did the child die?", options:["The seventh day","The third day","The fortieth day","At the new moon"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:18",verse:"And it came to pass on the seventh day, that the child died. And the servants of David feared to tell him that the child was dead: for they said, Behold, while the child was yet alive, we spake unto him, and he would not hearken unto our voice: how will he then vex himself, if we tell him that the child is dead?", insight:"Seven days of pleading, then silence; God heard every prayer and answered no."},
{q:"What did David do when he learned the child was dead?", options:["He arose, washed, anointed himself, and worshipped in the house of the LORD","He tore his clothes and cursed","He shut himself in his chamber","He sent for Nathan again"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:20",verse:"Then David arose from the earth, and washed, and anointed himself, and changed his apparel, and came into the house of the LORD, and worshipped: then he came to his own house; and when he required, they set bread before him, and he did eat.", insight:"He worshipped; grief that bows is stronger than grief that breaks."},
{q:"What reason had David given for fasting while the child lived?", options:["Who can tell whether GOD will be gracious to me, that the child may live?","To move the elders","To fulfill a vow","To punish himself"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:22",verse:"And he said, While the child was yet alive, I fasted and wept: for I said, Who can tell whether GOD will be gracious to me, that the child may live?", insight:"Hope prayed while hope was possible; David fasted for mercy, not to bargain with it."},
{q:"What comfort did David speak over the dead child?", options:["I shall go to him, but he shall not return to me","He is with Uriah now","The LORD is cruel","My son is lost forever"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:23",verse:"But now he is dead, wherefore should I fast? can I bring him back again? I shall go to him, but he shall not return to me.", insight:"I shall go to him; even in loss, David saw a road that did not end at the grave."},
{q:"What did David do for Bathsheba, and what son was born?", options:["He comforted her, and she bare a son, and he called his name Solomon","He sent her away","He gave her to another","He built her a new house"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:24",verse:"And David comforted Bath-sheba his wife, and went in unto her, and lay with her: and she bare a son, and he called his name Solomon: and the LORD loved him.", insight:"Comfort after loss; God can bring new life out of the ruins of old sin."},
{q:"What name did the LORD give Solomon through Nathan?", options:["Jedidiah, because of the LORD","Nathan, after the prophet","David, after his father","Peace, for the kingdom"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:25",verse:"And he sent by the hand of Nathan the prophet; and he called his name Jedidiah, because of the LORD.", insight:"Jedidiah means beloved of the LORD; grace renamed the child of the scandal."},
{q:"While David was in Jerusalem, what city did Joab take?", options:["Rabbah of the children of Ammon, the royal city","The city of David","Hebron","Ziklag"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 12:26",verse:"And Joab fought against Rabbah of the children of Ammon, and took the royal city.", insight:"The war David had stayed home from was won by his servants; grace finished what his guilt had abandoned."}
]},
{n:13, name:"Amnon and Tamar", seal:{ref:"2 Samuel 13:21",verse:"But when king David heard of all these things, he was very wroth."}, facts:["Did you know Tamar's plea, that no such thing ought to be done in Israel, marks Amnon's crime as a covenant violation, not only a personal wrong (2 Samuel 13:12)?","Did you know Absalom waited two full years before avenging his sister, nursing his hatred in silence while his father did nothing (2 Samuel 13:22, 13:23)?","Did you know the first report David heard was false, that Absalom had slain all the king's sons, when only Amnon was dead (2 Samuel 13:30, 13:32)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who fell in love with Tamar, Absalom's sister?", options:["Amnon, David's son","Jonadab, his friend","Chileab, his brother","Adonijah, his brother"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:1",verse:"And it came to pass after this, that Absalom the son of David had a fair sister, whose name was Tamar; and Amnon the son of David loved her.", insight:"Love in name only; what Amnon called love, the story will prove was lust."},
{q:"What happened to Amnon over his sister Tamar?", options:["He fell sick, vexed that she was a virgin","He left Jerusalem","He confessed to David","He married another"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:2",verse:"And Amnon was so vexed, that he fell sick for his sister Tamar; for she was a virgin; and Amnon thought it hard for him to do any thing to her.", insight:"Desire nursed in secret becomes a sickness; Amnon fed his thoughts until they mastered him."},
{q:"Who was Amnon's friend and counselor?", options:["Jonadab, the son of Shimeah, a very subtle man","Ahithophel the Gilonite","Hushai the Archite","Joab the captain"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:3",verse:"But Amnon had a friend, whose name was Jonadab, the son of Shimeah David’s brother: and Jonadab was a very subtil man.", insight:"A very subtle man; beware the friend whose cleverness serves your worst impulses."},
{q:"What plan did Jonadab give Amnon?", options:["Feign sickness, and ask the king to send Tamar to make him food","Flee to Geshur","Write Tamar a letter","Ask David for her hand"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:5",verse:"And Jonadab said unto him, Lay thee down on thy bed, and make thyself sick: and when thy father cometh to see thee, say unto him, I pray thee, let my sister Tamar come, and give me meat, and dress the meat in my sight, that I may see it, and eat it at her hand.", insight:"Sin loves a scheme; Jonadab dressed a trap as a sickbed request."},
{q:"What did David tell Tamar to do?", options:["Go to Amnon's house, and dress him meat","Stay in her chamber","Flee to Absalom","Call the guards"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:7",verse:"Then David sent home to Tamar, saying, Go now to thy brother Amnon’s house, and dress him meat.", insight:"David sent his daughter into danger unknowing; the king who once schemed could not see another's scheme."},
{q:"What did Amnon do before his crime?", options:["He put every man out from him","He locked the city gate","He sent for David","He called his brothers"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:9",verse:"And she took a pan, and poured them out before him; but he refused to eat. And Amnon said, Have out all men from me. And they went out every man from him.", insight:"He cleared the room; wickedness loves privacy, and the privacy itself should have warned everyone."},
{q:"What did Amnon say when he took hold of Tamar?", options:["Come lie with me, my sister","Forgive me, my sister","Leave this house","Call thy brother"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:11",verse:"And when she had brought them unto him to eat, he took hold of her, and said unto her, Come lie with me, my sister.", insight:"Plain and terrible; Scripture records the words without embroidery, and neither should we."},
{q:"How did Tamar answer him?", options:["Do not force me, for no such thing ought to be done in Israel","She agreed willingly","She laughed at him","She called for Jonadab"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:12",verse:"And she answered him, Nay, my brother, do not force me; for no such thing ought to be done in Israel: do not thou this folly.", insight:"Her plea was clear and righteous; no was spoken, and heaven heard it."},
{q:"What lawful path did Tamar point to?", options:["Speak to the king, for he will not withhold me from thee","Flee to Egypt","Appeal to the priests","Wait a year"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:13",verse:"And I, whither shall I cause my shame to go? and as for thee, thou shalt be as one of the fools in Israel. Now therefore, I pray thee, speak unto the king; for he will not withhold me from thee.", insight:"She offered an honorable way; lust would rather steal than ask."},
{q:"What did Amnon do despite her plea?", options:["He would not listen, but being stronger than she, forced her","He released her","He repented and wept","He sent her to Absalom"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:14",verse:"Howbeit he would not hearken unto her voice: but, being stronger than she, forced her, and lay with her.", insight:"Scripture names the violence plainly and lays the guilt where it belongs, on him."},
{q:"What followed Amnon's act?", options:["He hated her with a hatred greater than the love wherewith he had loved her","He married her","He begged forgiveness","He fled the city"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:15",verse:"Then Amnon hated her exceedingly; so that the hatred wherewith he hated her was greater than the love wherewith he had loved her. And Amnon said unto her, Arise, be gone.", insight:"Lust consumes its object; what he called love curdled into hatred the moment it was spent."},
{q:"What did Amnon order his servant to do?", options:["Put her out from him, and bolt the door after her","Bring her food","Call a physician","Escort her to David"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:17",verse:"Then he called his servant that ministered unto him, and said, Put now this woman out from me, and bolt the door after her.", insight:"Cruelty upon cruelty; having wronged her, he would not even let her remain."},
{q:"How did Tamar show her grief?", options:["She put ashes on her head, rent her garment, and went on crying","She fled to the wilderness","She cursed Amnon","She hid in the palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:19",verse:"And Tamar put ashes on her head, and rent her garment of divers colours that was on her, and laid her hand on her head, and went on crying.", insight:"Ashes and a torn robe; her grief was public because her wound was real."},
{q:"What did Absalom say to his sister?", options:["Hath Amnon thy brother been with thee? Hold now thy peace, my sister","Go and accuse him","Flee to Geshur","I will kill him today"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:20",verse:"And Absalom her brother said unto her, Hath Amnon thy brother been with thee? but hold now thy peace, my sister: he is thy brother; regard not this thing. So Tamar remained desolate in her brother Absalom’s house.", insight:"Regard not this thing; Absalom counseled silence, but silence was already plotting revenge."},
{q:"How did David respond to the crime?", options:["He was very wroth","He banished Amnon","He executed Amnon","He ignored it wholly"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:21",verse:"But when king David heard of all these things, he was very wroth.", insight:"Wrath without action; a father's anger that never becomes justice leaves wounds to fester."},
{q:"What was Absalom's outward manner toward Amnon?", options:["He spake unto him neither good nor bad","He cursed him daily","He embraced him","He challenged him"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:22",verse:"And Absalom spake unto his brother Amnon neither good nor bad: for Absalom hated Amnon, because he had forced his sister Tamar.", insight:"Neither good nor bad; the quietest hatred is often the most patient, and the most dangerous."},
{q:"How long did Absalom wait before his revenge?", options:["Two full years","Forty days","Seven years","One month"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:23",verse:"And it came to pass after two full years, that Absalom had sheepshearers in Baal-hazor, which is beside Ephraim: and Absalom invited all the king’s sons.", insight:"Two full years; revenge kept its calendar while David's justice slept."},
{q:"What order did Absalom give his servants at the sheepshearing feast?", options:["When Amnon is merry with wine, smite him; fear not","Arrest him at dawn","Poison his cup","Spare him"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:28",verse:"Now Absalom had commanded his servants, saying, Mark ye now when Amnon’s heart is merry with wine, and when I say unto you, Smite Amnon; then kill him, fear not: have not I commanded you? be courageous and be valiant.", insight:"Murder dressed as a feast; Absalom answered one violence with another, and the house of David bled again."},
{q:"What report first reached David?", options:["Absalom hath slain all the king's sons","Amnon is dead alone","Absalom has fled to Egypt","The servants have rebelled"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:30",verse:"And it came to pass, while they were in the way, that tidings came to David, saying, Absalom hath slain all the king’s sons, and there is not one of them left.", insight:"The first report was worse than the truth; panic always outruns the facts."},
{q:"Where did Absalom flee after killing Amnon?", options:["To Talmai, the son of Ammihud, king of Geshur","To Egypt","To the Philistines","To Rabbah"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 13:37",verse:"But Absalom fled, and went to Talmai, the son of Ammihud, king of Geshur. And David mourned for his son every day.", insight:"To his mother's people in Geshur; the avenger became the exile, and David's house was broken in two."}
]},
{n:14, name:"Absalom Brought Home", seal:{ref:"2 Samuel 14:14",verse:"For we must needs die, and are as water spilt on the ground, which cannot be gathered up again; neither doth God respect any person: yet doth he devise means, that his banished be not expelled from him."}, facts:["Did you know the wise woman of Tekoah is one of the few women in Scripture called wise, hired by Joab to do with a parable what no counselor dared say plainly (2 Samuel 14:2)?","Did you know Absalom's yearly haircut weighed two hundred shekels, about five pounds, a detail Scripture preserves as a portrait of his pride (2 Samuel 14:26)?","Did you know David's pardon was only half a pardon: Absalom could live in Jerusalem but not see the king's face for two full years (2 Samuel 14:24, 14:28)?"], questions:[
{q:"Whom did Joab send to fetch for his plan?", options:["A wise woman of Tekoah","A prophet from Shiloh","An elder of Judah","A scribe from Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:2",verse:"And Joab sent to Tekoah, and fetched thence a wise woman, and said unto her, I pray thee, feign thyself to be a mourner, and put on now mourning apparel, and anoint not thyself with oil, but be as a woman that had a long time mourned for the dead:", insight:"Joab reached for a storyteller; like Nathan before him, he knew a parable could move a king."},
{q:"What did Joab do with the woman?", options:["He put words in her mouth to speak to the king","He sent her with gold","He told her only to weep","He gave her a letter"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:3",verse:"And come to the king, and speak on this manner unto him. So Joab put the words in her mouth.", insight:"Borrowed words for a borrowed grief; Joab staged the sorrow to steer the king."},
{q:"In the woman's story, what happened to her two sons?", options:["They strove together in the field, and one smote the other and slew him","Both died of plague","One fled to Egypt","Both were taken by soldiers"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:6",verse:"And thy handmaid had two sons, and they two strove together in the field, and there was none to part them, but the one smote the other, and slew him.", insight:"A brother's blood on a brother's hands; the fiction mirrored the fact too closely to miss."},
{q:"What did the family demand in her story?", options:["Deliver the murderer, that they may kill him","Pay a ransom","Exile them both","Burn the field"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:7",verse:"And, behold, the whole family is risen against thine handmaid, and they said, Deliver him that smote his brother, that we may kill him, for the life of his brother whom he slew; and we will destroy the heir also: and so they shall quench my coal which is left, and shall not leave to my husband neither name nor remainder upon the earth.", insight:"The family's justice would quench her last coal; vengeance pursued fully leaves the house dark."},
{q:"What oath did the king swear to the woman?", options:["As the LORD liveth, not one hair of thy son shall fall to the earth","He would pay her silver","Her son would be exiled","The family would be punished"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:11",verse:"Then said she, I pray thee, let the king remember the LORD thy God, that thou wouldest not suffer the revengers of blood to destroy any more, lest they destroy my son. And he said, As the LORD liveth, there shall not one hair of thy son fall to the earth.", insight:"Not one hair; David could swear mercy for a stranger's son while his own son sat in exile."},
{q:"How did the woman turn the parable back on David?", options:["Wherefore then hast thou thought such a thing against the people of God, in not fetching home his banished?","Why hast thou forsaken the LORD?","Where is thy son Amnon?","Who made thee judge?"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:13",verse:"And the woman said, Wherefore then hast thou thought such a thing against the people of God? for the king doth speak this thing as one which is faulty, in that the king doth not fetch home again his banished.", insight:"The trap of the parable snapped shut; David had condemned a fiction and must now free a son."},
{q:"What picture did the woman paint of human life?", options:["We must needs die, and are as water spilt on the ground","We are as grass that withers","We are as sheep gone astray","We are as dust in the wind"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:14",verse:"For we must needs die, and are as water spilt on the ground, which cannot be gathered up again; neither doth God respect any person: yet doth he devise means, that his banished be not expelled from him.", insight:"Water spilt cannot be gathered; since life is short, mercy must be swift."},
{q:"What did the woman say God devises?", options:["Means, that His banished be not expelled from Him","New laws for kings","A temple in Jerusalem","War against His enemies"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:14",verse:"For we must needs die, and are as water spilt on the ground, which cannot be gathered up again; neither doth God respect any person: yet doth he devise means, that his banished be not expelled from him.", insight:"God Himself finds a way to bring the banished home; mercy is not man's invention but God's design."},
{q:"To whom did the woman compare the king?", options:["An angel of God","A prophet of old","A father to Israel","A lion in battle"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:17",verse:"Then thine handmaid said, The word of my lord the king shall now be comfortable: for as an angel of God, so is my lord the king to discern good and bad: therefore the LORD thy God will be with thee.", insight:"Flattery opened the door the parable had unlocked; even wise words can carry a little honey."},
{q:"What did David ask the woman?", options:["Is not the hand of Joab with thee in all this?","Who sent thee hither?","Where is thy husband?","Why weepest thou?"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:19",verse:"And the king said, Is not the hand of Joab with thee in all this? And the woman answered and said, As thy soul liveth, my lord the king, none can turn to the right hand or to the left from ought that my lord the king hath spoken: for thy servant Joab, he bade me, and he put all these words in the mouth of thine handmaid:", insight:"David saw through the performance; a king who could be moved by a story could still spot the hand behind it."},
{q:"Why did the woman say Joab had done all this?", options:["To fetch about the form of this speech","To seize the throne","To avenge Amnon","To enrich Tekoah"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:20",verse:"To fetch about this form of speech hath thy servant Joab done this thing: and my lord is wise, according to the wisdom of an angel of God, to know all things that are in the earth.", insight:"To fetch about the form of this speech; Joab wanted said aloud what no one dared say plainly."},
{q:"What command did David give Joab?", options:["Go now, bring the young man Absalom again","Banish Absalom forever","Execute Absalom","Forget Absalom"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:21",verse:"And the king said unto Joab, Behold now, I have done this thing: go therefore, bring the young man Absalom again.", insight:"Bring the young man Absalom again; the king's heart had been ready long before his word."},
{q:"From where did Joab fetch Absalom?", options:["Geshur","Egypt","Hebron","Mahanaim"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:23",verse:"So Joab arose and went to Geshur, and brought Absalom to Jerusalem.", insight:"Three years in Geshur ended with a summons; exile is long, but a father's call can end it in a day."},
{q:"What condition did David set on Absalom's return?", options:["Let him turn to his own house, and let him not see my face","He must live in Hebron","He must pay tribute","He must shave his head"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:24",verse:"And the king said, Let him turn to his own house, and let him not see my face. So Absalom returned to his own house, and saw not the king’s face.", insight:"Home without welcome; David opened the city but closed the door, and half a pardon bred a whole rebellion."},
{q:"How is Absalom's appearance described?", options:["In all Israel there was none so much praised for beauty, with no blemish from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head","Short and plain","Scarred from battle","Grey before his time"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:25",verse:"But in all Israel there was none to be so much praised as Absalom for his beauty: from the sole of his foot even to the crown of his head there was no blemish in him.", insight:"Beauty without blemish; the chapter lingers on his looks, and looks would become his snare."},
{q:"How heavy was Absalom's hair when he cut it at every year's end?", options:["Two hundred shekels","Fifty shekels","One talent","A hundred shekels"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:26",verse:"And when he polled his head, (for it was at every year’s end that he polled it: because the hair was heavy on him, therefore he polled it:) he weighed the hair of his head at two hundred shekels after the king’s weight.", insight:"Two hundred shekels of hair; vanity kept its own accounts, and pride was already weighing itself."},
{q:"What children did Absalom have?", options:["Three sons, and one daughter whose name was Tamar","Seven sons","One son only","Two daughters"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:27",verse:"And unto Absalom there were born three sons, and one daughter, whose name was Tamar: she was a woman of a fair countenance.", insight:"He named his daughter Tamar; the sister he avenged lived on in his own house."},
{q:"How long did Absalom live in Jerusalem without seeing the king's face?", options:["Two full years","Forty days","Seven years","Six months"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:28",verse:"So Absalom dwelt two full years in Jerusalem, and saw not the king’s face.", insight:"Two years at home, still banished; nearness without favor is its own kind of exile."},
{q:"What did Absalom do to get Joab's attention?", options:["He set Joab's barley field on fire","He sent him gold","He wrote to the king","He seized the gate"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:30",verse:"Therefore he said unto his servants, See, Joab’s field is near mine, and he hath barley there; go and set it on fire. And Absalom’s servants set the field on fire.", insight:"He burned a field to start a conversation; Absalom always reached for the dramatic, never the humble."},
{q:"How did David receive Absalom at last?", options:["The king kissed Absalom","He rebuked him","He wept aloud","He turned away"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 14:33",verse:"So Joab came to the king, and told him: and when he had called for Absalom, he came to the king, and bowed himself on his face to the ground before the king: and the king kissed Absalom.", insight:"A kiss, but no words recorded; reconciliation without repentance is a truce, not a peace."}
]},
{n:15, name:"Absalom's Rebellion", seal:{ref:"2 Samuel 15:6",verse:"And on this manner did Absalom to all Israel that came to the king for judgment: so Absalom stole the hearts of the men of Israel."}, facts:["Did you know Absalom spent years at the city gate, agreeing with every man's complaint and kissing those who bowed, a long campaign that stole the hearts of the men of Israel (2 Samuel 15:2 to 15:6)?","Did you know David refused to carry the ark into exile as a good luck charm, sending it back with the words, let him do to me as seemeth good unto him (2 Samuel 15:25, 15:26)?","Did you know the six hundred Gittites, Philistine soldiers from Gath, stayed loyal to David when his own nation turned, marching out with him past the Kidron (2 Samuel 15:18 to 15:22)?"], questions:[
{q:"What did Absalom prepare for himself?", options:["Chariots and horses, and fifty men to run before him","A new palace","An army of Philistines","A fleet of ships"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:1",verse:"And it came to pass after this, that Absalom prepared him chariots and horses, and fifty men to run before him.", insight:"Fifty runners before him; Absalom staged a kingship long before he claimed one."},
{q:"Where did Absalom station himself early each morning?", options:["Beside the way of the gate","At the temple steps","In the marketplace","On the city wall"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:2",verse:"And Absalom rose up early, and stood beside the way of the gate: and it was so, that when any man that had a controversy came to the king for judgment, then Absalom called unto him, and said, Of what city art thou? And he said, Thy servant is of one of the tribes of Israel.", insight:"Early at the gate; ambition keeps better hours than contentment."},
{q:"What did Absalom tell each man who came with a case?", options:["Thy matters are good and right, but there is no man deputed of the king to hear thee","The king will judge thee","Go home in peace","Pay the fine"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:3",verse:"And Absalom said unto him, See, thy matters are good and right; but there is no man deputed of the king to hear thee.", insight:"He agreed with every complaint; flattery is the cheapest way to steal a heart."},
{q:"What did Absalom wish aloud?", options:["Oh that I were made judge in the land!","That David would return","That Joab were removed","That the ark would speak"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:4",verse:"Absalom said moreover, Oh that I were made judge in the land, that every man which hath any suit or cause might come unto me, and I would do him justice!", insight:"That every man might come unto me; he campaigned on the promise of being heard."},
{q:"What did Absalom do when men bowed down to him?", options:["He put forth his hand, and took hold of them, and kissed them","He rebuked them","He ignored them","He reported them"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:5",verse:"And it was so, that when any man came nigh to him to do him obeisance, he put forth his hand, and took him, and kissed him.", insight:"A kiss instead of a bow; Absalom traded royal dignity for popularity, and it paid."},
{q:"What does Scripture say Absalom did to Israel?", options:["He stole the hearts of the men of Israel","He bought their loyalty","He frightened them","He taught them"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:6",verse:"And on this manner did Absalom to all Israel that came to the king for judgment: so Absalom stole the hearts of the men of Israel.", insight:"Stole the hearts; the verb is theft, not persuasion. Stolen loyalty is still stolen."},
{q:"What vow did Absalom claim he must pay in Hebron?", options:["If the LORD had brought him back to Jerusalem, he would serve the LORD","To build an altar","To fast forty days","To free the captives"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:8",verse:"For thy servant vowed a vow while I abode at Geshur in Syria, saying, If the LORD shall bring me again indeed to Jerusalem, then I will serve the LORD.", insight:"He wrapped treason in a vow; the most dangerous lies wear the clothes of worship."},
{q:"What were Absalom's spies to proclaim at the sound of the trumpet?", options:["Absalom reigneth in Hebron","David is dead","The ark is taken","Peace in Israel"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:10",verse:"But Absalom sent spies throughout all the tribes of Israel, saying, As soon as ye hear the sound of the trumpet, then ye shall say, Absalom reigneth in Hebron.", insight:"One trumpet blast, one sentence; rebellion needs only a signal and a slogan."},
{q:"Who went with Absalom out of Jerusalem?", options:["Two hundred men, who went in their simplicity, and knew not any thing","His mighty men","The priests","The king's guard"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:11",verse:"And with Absalom went two hundred men out of Jerusalem, that were called; and they went in their simplicity, and they knew not any thing.", insight:"Two hundred innocent companions; a schemer's crowd is often borrowed, not bought."},
{q:"Whom did Absalom summon from Giloh?", options:["Ahithophel the Gilonite, David's counsellor","Hushai the Archite","Zadok the priest","Ittai the Gittite"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:12",verse:"And Absalom sent for Ahithophel the Gilonite, David’s counsellor, from his city, even from Giloh, while he offered sacrifices. And the conspiracy was strong; for the people increased continually with Absalom.", insight:"He stole David's wisest counselor; betrayal hurts most when it knows the way in."},
{q:"What news did the messenger bring to David?", options:["The hearts of the men of Israel are after Absalom","Absalom is dead","Hebron has fallen","Ahithophel is coming"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:13",verse:"And there came a messenger to David, saying, The hearts of the men of Israel are after Absalom.", insight:"The hearts Absalom stole now marched; David heard in one sentence what four years of kisses had built."},
{q:"What did David order when he heard the news?", options:["Arise, and let us flee","Arm for battle","Bar the gates","Send for Absalom"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:14",verse:"And David said unto all his servants that were with him at Jerusalem, Arise, and let us flee; for we shall not else escape from Absalom: make speed to depart, lest he overtake us suddenly, and bring evil upon us, and smite the city with the edge of the sword.", insight:"Lest he smite the city with the edge of the sword; David chose flight over a siege that would slaughter Jerusalem."},
{q:"Who marched with David out of Jerusalem?", options:["The Cherethites, and the Pelethites, and six hundred Gittites","The tribe of Judah","The Ammonites","The elders of the city"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:18",verse:"And all his servants passed on beside him; and all the Cherethites, and all the Pelethites, and all the Gittites, six hundred men which came after him from Gath, passed on before the king.", insight:"Six hundred foreigners stayed when Israel left; loyalty is proven in the leaving."},
{q:"How did Ittai the Gittite answer David's urging to turn back?", options:["Where my lord the king shall be, there will thy servant be","I will return to Gath","Give me gold","I fear Absalom"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:21",verse:"And Ittai answered the king, and said, As the LORD liveth, and as my lord the king liveth, surely in what place my lord the king shall be, whether in death or life, even there also will thy servant be.", insight:"A Philistine outswore Israel; sometimes the stranger keeps the covenant the sons break."},
{q:"What did the countryside do as David left?", options:["All the country wept with a loud voice","They cheered Absalom","They shut their doors","They fled to the hills"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:23",verse:"And all the country wept with a loud voice, and all the people passed over: the king also himself passed over the brook Kidron, and all the people passed over, toward the way of the wilderness.", insight:"The land wept; even the country felt the wound of a son risen against his father."},
{q:"What did David tell Zadok to do with the ark?", options:["Carry back the ark of God into the city","Hide it in a cave","Take it to Gath","Burn incense before it"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:25",verse:"And the king said unto Zadok, Carry back the ark of God into the city: if I shall find favour in the eyes of the LORD, he will bring me again, and shew me both it, and his habitation:", insight:"David would not use God as a charm; the ark belonged in Jerusalem, whatever became of the king."},
{q:"What did David say if the LORD had no delight in him?", options:["Behold, here am I, let Him do to me as seemeth good unto Him","Then I will fight","Then I will hide","Then I will despair"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:26",verse:"But if he thus say, I have no delight in thee; behold, here am I, let him do to me as seemeth good unto him.", insight:"Here am I; surrender, not strategy, was David's last word on the road out."},
{q:"How did David climb the mount of Olives?", options:["Barefoot, and with his head covered, and weeping","On horseback in armor","Singing psalms","Carried by his servants"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:30",verse:"And David went up by the ascent of mount Olivet, and wept as he went up, and had his head covered, and he went barefoot: and all the people that was with him covered every man his head, and they went up, weeping as they went up.", insight:"Barefoot and weeping; the king who danced before the ark now climbed in tears, and God was with him still."},
{q:"What did David pray when he heard Ahithophel had joined Absalom?", options:["O LORD, I pray thee, turn the counsel of Ahithophel into foolishness","Slay Ahithophel","Give me a new counselor","Bring back the ark"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:31",verse:"And one told David, saying, Ahithophel is among the conspirators with Absalom. And David said, O LORD, I pray thee, turn the counsel of Ahithophel into foolishness.", insight:"One short prayer against the wisest head in Israel; David knew battles are won in heaven first."},
{q:"Who met David at the top of the mount?", options:["Hushai the Archite, with his coat rent and earth upon his head","Ahithophel","Zadok","Ittai"], a:0, ref:"2 Samuel 15:32",verse:"And it came to pass, that when David was come to the top of the mount, where he worshipped God, behold, Hushai the Archite came to meet him with his coat rent, and earth upon his head:", insight:"A friend in torn clothes; God sent David counsel in the very hour his counsel was stolen."}
]}
]);
